-- title:
--   Anharmonic involution μ↦ 1/16-μ on the λ-field
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime with $q \neq 2$ and let $K$ be an intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q}$ in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`. Write $\mu =$ `lambdaModC (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)` for the Laurent series over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ obtained by coefficientwise base change from the integral series `lambdaInt` (the product of $\mathfrak{q}$, the eighth power of the $\eta$-product series, the substitution $\mathfrak{q} \mapsto \mathfrak{q}^4$ applied to its sixteenth power, and the substitution $\mathfrak{q} \mapsto \mathfrak{q}^2$ applied to `dedekindEtaUnitInv`), and $\mu_q =$ `lambdaNModC (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) q` for the image of $\mu$ under the substitution $\mathfrak{q} \mapsto \mathfrak{q}^q$. Let $F =$ `lambdaFieldOver q K` be the subfield of `LaurentSeries (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)` generated by the constant series with coefficients in $K$ together with $\mu$ and $\mu_q$. The assertion is that there exists a ring automorphism $\sigma$ of $F$ such that: $\sigma$ fixes every element of $F$ whose underlying Laurent series is a constant series coming from $K$; $\sigma(\mu) = 16^{-1} - \mu$; and $\sigma(\mu_q) = 16^{-1} - \mu_q$, the last two as equalities of the underlying Laurent series, $16^{-1}$ being the inverse of $16$ in the Laurent series field.
--
--   In classical terms this is the anharmonic substitution $\lambda \mapsto 1 - \lambda$ on the Legendre modular function, normalised as $\mu = \lambda/16$, realised simultaneously on level $1$ and level $q$ as an automorphism of the field generated over $K$ by $\mu(\mathfrak{q})$ and $\mu(\mathfrak{q}^q)$. It feeds the construction of the full anharmonic action on this field and the transport of that action to values of $\lambda$, which are its two downstream consumers.
-- source:
